  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>From Cabasson beach, the coastal path runs along the coast opposite Fort de Brégançon, between beaches, coves and pine woods. This is the "Plage de Cabasson – Plage du Pellegrin" section: 7.7 km in a classified site. The Bormes tourist office gives 6 h for the full route.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>According to the FAQ of the Bormes-les-Mimosas tourist office, dogs are accepted on hikes on a leash. Beaches have their own rules: from 15 June to 30 September, the town allows leashed dogs only on the part of <a href="/en/fiche-plage-cabasson-bormes-les-mimosas">Cabasson beach</a> near the Fort (east side of the rocky spur) and on Gouron beach, the other municipal beaches being reserved for working dogs. See also the <a href="/en/fiche-bormes-les-mimosas">Fort de Brégançon beach</a>. At the Estagnol, pets are strictly prohibited inside the property (paid park).</p>
      <p>For parking, the Parc Capalo (private) is paid from April to October: €12 per car from 8:30am to 7pm according to the town. Outside this period, Cabasson parking is free.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: in summer, check the beach area allowed for dogs before parking, and keep your dog on a leash along the whole path.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>Fort de Brégançon dominates the landscape, and Bormes-les-Mimosas is only a few minutes away to extend the walk.</p>
    </div>

